WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.977
When plasma osmolality increases, or circulating volume falls, the body

00:00:04.977 --> 00:00:07.465
signals for antidiuretic hormone, ADH.

00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:11.598
Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus sense the concentration of the plasma.

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:14.870
The hypothalamus also synthesizes ADH.

00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:18.123
The hormone is stored in the posterior pituitary, then released

00:00:18.123 --> 00:00:18.965
into the blood.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.243
Keep those locations separate: synthesis in the hypothalamus, storage and

00:00:23.243 --> 00:00:25.100
release from the posterior pituitary.

00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:27.550
Now follow the blood to the kidney.

00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:31.648
ADH acts on the renal collecting ducts and increases their

00:00:31.648 --> 00:00:32.742
permeability to water.

00:00:33.364 --> 00:00:35.612
More water is reabsorbed into the blood.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.572
That is the link between the hormone signal and the

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:40.193
fluid response.

00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:41.964
Compare that with diabetes insipidus.

00:00:42.514 --> 00:00:45.547
In the neurogenic form, there is insufficient ADH.

00:00:45.942 --> 00:00:49.465
In the nephrogenic form, the collecting tubules are insensitive to

00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:49.817
ADH.

00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:52.839
Both can leave the patient unable to concentrate urine.

00:00:53.463 --> 00:00:56.237
The same result can come from a supply problem or

00:00:56.237 --> 00:00:57.069
a response problem.
